[{:seon.task/name "prove-runs-what-a-change-reaches"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged; AGENTS.md §3 Testing and §5, results reused by input digest; design: notes/archive/fork-test-merge-design-2026-10-22.md §2): one of each. The selection `seon.proofs/plan`: a key runs exactly when no green is stored at its reach digest; with a base (an agent's branch against main at its merge base) it selects the proofs and the moved digests, without one the proofs and every key with no green. The digests `seon.program/reach-digests` (tests by default; bin/check steps by their seon.check functions through `seon.proofs/check-steps`, the one list bin/check reads from `./seon steps`; `seon.proofs/step-digests` names a step whose function is gone as missing, red). The runner `seon.proofs/run-tests!` (bin/test's shards and an agent's test fork). The results directory `:seon.config.source/test-results-dir` (`seon.proofs/greens`, `seon.proofs/store!` through `seon.fx/write-file`). Entry points: `bin/prove` for worktree sessions and root (`bin/test` and `bin/check` without arguments are its halves; `bin/check --steps DIR`), and `seon.agent/test-plan` for an agent's run (the turn's observe, the reconciler's :tests, the merge gate, `(my.agent/prove)`); full runs are explicit (`bin/test --all`, `bin/check 100`); a merge runs bin/prove on the merged tree (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches, with the measurements). Deleted: `bin/test --reach` and test-runner's plan, run-vars!, reach-main and green?; `seon.agent/test-vars`; `seon.program/reaching-tests`. Shards are processes bin/test starts, not forks of the runner (a forked child of the jolt test runtime hangs at its first d/connect). The digest's inputs: quoted names by namespace, `seon.program/env-readers`, a test's form reached only as its own root, system.edn's procs as data refs (`seon.fx/declared-refs`), a test running the binary only through `seon.test-store/binary-sh`, and the config in force (`test-runner/config-input`). Agents: the source tree's inputs (`seon.fx/inputs`, the one producer, also bin/test's) are read by `seon.fx/read-source` on an effect worker and written on the boot's `:seon.root/source` fact (`seon.root/import-source!`) only when the running binary is built from that tree (`target/seon.tree`, `seon.fx/tree-id`), else `:unshared` says why; an agent uses only its root's current boot's inputs and keeps its greens in the shared results directory (:seon.config.source/test-results-dir) through root's :file owner: the owner ruled (2026-10-23) that a test that is the same is not run again once green, \"especially when freshly branched\", so an agent's greens and a checkout's are one store (seon.agent-test/an-agents-greens-and-a-checkouts-are-one-store). A shard compares the planned tree before storing, so an edit during a run never stores a green under the unedited digests. v1 keyed a result by its reach digest (~/src/seon seon.test.cache). Open: shards are balanced by test count, not time; seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes waits on an `nc -w 600` that never times out an idle connection on macOS."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/refs-name-quoted-symbols-and-macro-uses"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/an-unchanged-tree-runs-nothing-and-reuses-everything
                      seon.proofs-test/a-one-defn-change-runs-only-what-reaches-it
                      seon.proofs-test/a-macro-schema-or-config-change-widens-the-selection
                      seon.proofs-test/a-check-step-is-keyed-by-what-its-function-reaches
                      seon.proofs-test/shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored
                      seon.proofs-test/selection-is-proportional-to-what-changed
                      seon.program-test/only-a-test-reading-the-tree-takes-its-digest
                      seon.program-test/the-same-tree-gives-the-same-digests-in-any-checkout-and-any-run
                      seon.program-test/a-test-naming-another-test-does-not-reach-it
                      seon.proofs-test/a-failed-write-or-a-throwing-fixture-is-recorded-red
                      seon.agent-test/a-wide-reach-reuses-unchanged-results}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-judge-reads-run-tests-results"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent's run carries clojure.test's report format end to end: `seon.agent/run-tests-here` collects each assertion as a report map (`seon.agent/test-report`), and the judge and the turn read them back through `seon.proofs/results`, `seon.proofs/red-reports`, `seon.proofs/failures`, `seon.proofs/test-run-errors` and `seon.proofs/costs`, while `seon.proofs/run-tests!` already answers one result per test ({:test :digest :pass :fail :error :ms}, a throw's :message and :data). Correct: the judge (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`'s input), the turn (`seon.turn/observe`), the run's facts (:seon.run/failures, :seon.run/costs) and the context read run-tests!'s result map, each red test keeping its failing assertions (expected, actual, got); the report vector, `test-report` and the readers that rebuild a status per test from it go. About -30 lines in src. Proofs: the done error, the run's failures and costs, and the context's red block read the same from the result map as today from the reports."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.proofs/prove-runs-what-a-change-reaches"}}
 {:seon.task/name "turn-logic-from-recorded-data"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: tests shouldn't be running agent turns. A run that names nothing leaves out the tests and steps that reach a process (test-runner/automatic over seon.program/reach-index's :process?; notes/process-tests-2026-10-23.md), so what they cover is proven only when named, in the full run or at root's gate. Where the covered logic is a function of data, test it without a process: the reconciler's step from a recorded fact state (seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc on a db holding the run facts, as procs-test does), the merge from two dbs (seon.merge/three-way, global-ids' delta-three-way), the context render from a recorded basis (seon.context on a :seon.turn/basis), the agent-proc step from recorded events, seon.server/respond on recorded requests, the help page's and templates' forms through seon.repl's handler in process, the restart catch-up as load-all! over a db holding two merges. Each new unit test names the process test whose logic it carries; the process test stays as the end-to-end check, run when named. Measured: the process tests were 90 of 443 tests and 782 of their 917 s (2026-10-23 full run)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-nested-runs-reports-reach-only-its-run"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.proofs/run-tests!` counts its own tests through its report binding and passes every report on to clojure.test's default report (`seon.proofs/report`, the root value captured at load), so the outer binding is never used. That report bumps whatever tally is in force (Jolt's stdlib clojure/test.clj, `inc-report-counter` and `fail!`): an enclosing t/run-tests' *report-counters* when one is bound, else Jolt's process-wide `counters` atom (`global-tally?`). So a test that runs tests of its own, such as seon.proofs-test/shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored with its deliberate (is (= 1 2)) in app.pv-red-test, adds that fail to the caller's tally. Under t/run-tests the outer test reads red (found by pure-tests, 2026-10-23); under bin/test it is green, because test-runner reads run-tests!'s own counts, but Jolt's process tally (n-fail, failures) still carries the inner fail. Cause: the nested run has no tally of its own. Correct: run-tests! binds clojure.test/*report-counters* to a ref of its own around its test-vars, so a nested run's reports land in that ref and nowhere else (Jolt's global-tally? is false for a caller-bound ref); its results stay its volatiles. Replaces nothing; +1 binding. Proofs: shards-split… is green under t/run-tests as under bin/test, and Jolt's process tally reads the same before and after it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-reach-walk-a-plan"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23, task seon.derive/one-content-keyed-derivation's cold paths). What existed: test-runner/plan-of built the reach graph twice a plan, once in tests-of (seon.program/reach-index over every test) and again for the bin/check steps (reach-index with the steps' roots), though the graph (seon.program/reach-graph) is the same value and only seon.program/graph-index differs by roots. Measured on the whole tree (src and test, 2,769 forms): one reach-graph is 232M ticks and 912 MB. Fix at the cause: tests-of builds the graph once and hands it on (:graph), and plan-of reads the steps' index from it (graph-index). A plan of tests and steps: 2.88G ticks and 8.8 GB before, 2.65G ticks and 7.9 GB after (8% of the plan). What is left of the plan is the import into a db (seon.source/import-tx 510M ticks, 3.2 GB; d/db-with 1.9G ticks, 3.7 GB, the store session's). Replaces the second walk; deletes nothing else."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-plan-keeps-a-program-db-per-tree"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; the owner's option B, notes/test-fork-main-2026-10-23.md). What existed: test-runner/program-db imported the whole of src and test into the in-memory test store on every plan (seon.source/import-tx, then d/db-with): on today's main a plan of tests and steps was 1.94G ticks warm (6.1 s CPU), and transacting the import into LMDB alone 1.43G ticks (4.2 s) even after jolt/datom-cost. The program db is a function of the git tree, so it is kept per tree: one LMDB store per checkout (:seon.config.proofs/program-store, target/program-store), a branch per tree id (seon.fx/tree-id, git's content address, never updated). The same tree reads its branch; a tree never seen is branched from the kept tree with the fewest changed program files (git diff --name-only, trees in the checkout's own repository) and brought current by seon.source/refresh-tx; only an empty store imports the whole program. Every text is read from the tree's git objects (git archive), never the working tree, so an edit after the tree id was taken cannot be kept under it; the diff is --no-renames, so a moved and renamed namespace leaves no old namespace behind. One writer at a time per store (two runs never share a checkout). Bounded in bytes (:seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes): an LMDB file never shrinks, so past the bound the store is removed before it is opened and starts again from the run's tree. A new tree's nearest kept tree costs one git diff per kept tree (a few ms each). Measured: d/branch! 2 ms, a one-file refresh-tx 6 ms and its transact 23 ms; the store 12 MB for the whole program, ~2 MB more a kept tree with one changed file; a kept tree's program db 1.17M ticks and 4.6 MB whatever the program's size (the cost proof); bin/test's selection 1.65 s on an unchanged tree and 1.7 s after a one-file edit, against ~15 s with an empty store. What is left of a plan is the reach graph (~240M ticks, ~1 s). Tests that built a whole program db (program-test, agent-test, main-test) take this process's own store (seon.test-store/program-store). Deletes the per-run import and the runner's note that a fork cannot connect to a store. Option A, a worktree's root answering through seon_prove, stays the end state: this is its tree-to-branch step."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-plan-reads-the-kept-program-db-of-its-tree
                      seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-runs-the-binary-through-binary-sh"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session): seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-its-keys-under-their-current-names runs bin/ports, which runs ./seon ports, through clojure.java.shell/sh directly, not through seon.test-store/binary-sh, the one call seon.program/reach-index's :binary? counts (:seon.config.source/binary-vars). So bin/test's plan does not build target/seon for it, and on a worktree whose binary is another tree's it is red for a reason that is not the change (config refused: unknown keys). Correct: every test that runs the binary or a script that runs it (bin/root, bin/check, bin/ports, bin/mcp, ./seon) does so through binary-sh, and a law over test/'s forms (read as data) is red on a shell call naming one of them another way, as seon.check-test/every-test-root-starts-through-start-root! is for roots."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-prove-runs-key-does-not-move-between-two-asks"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (owner's ruling 2026-10-24: a prove run is keyed by the digest of what it reads). Cause: main's tx moved between the two asks by the run's own writes, its results and then its prove agent's end facts (status, ended-ms), after the tx seon.prove remembered (:after), so the second ask spawned a second run. A run's key is now branch@digest of what it reads: main's, the checkout's seon.fx/inputs (tree and env) and main's program (seon.program/program-digest, new: it replaces the :after rule, deleted); an agent's, its branch's program digest, which seon.root/prove! and seon.agent/prove! take of the same head (an agent's tx moves too: the eval that asked records itself on its branch). Any file change in the checkout (docs too) or change to main's program moves main's key, and a re-plan reuses every green; coarser than the per-test digests because it must be known before admission, which those cannot be without the run's own planning (spawn and load of the tests, 9-14 s measured). A repeat ask of main: 13.7 s -> 0.48 s. docs/merging.md#the-test-service. Original report: Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. `bin/test --all seon.prove-test`: `seon.prove-test/a-session-prove-of-main-runs-in-a-prove-agent-and-leaves-facts` (test/seon/prove_test.clj:157-158) asks the same prove twice and expects the same run: the first key is `main@536871112:seon.patterns-test`, the second `main@536871152:seon.patterns-test`, and two run facts exist where one is expected. Cause to find: the key's number (a transaction/entity id of main) moved between the two asks, so the run is not recognised as the same one. Correct: the key names the run by what it is run for (the digest of its inputs), never by a counter that moves when anything else is written; asked again with nothing changed the answer is the same run and nothing runs. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/main-is-proved-at-boot-with-the-config-on-nobody-asking seon.prove-test/a-run-is-keyed-by-what-it-reads seon.prove-test/a-session-and-an-agents-turn-for-one-digest-end-in-one-run seon.program-test/the-program-digest-moves-with-the-program-and-not-with-data seon.program-cost-test/the-program-digest-is-one-read-of-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-prove-path"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md, root's special cases go: one prove path). What existed: a proof run's tick budget was per run, two keys by mode (`:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` for the judge, a turn's own 10x smaller one), so a test's verdict depended on where it ran and on what ran before it in the run: a proof that needed more than the turn's budget was red (`:seon.test/ticks-spent` on the running test) in every turn and green at the judge, and a cheap test after a costly one was red in one run and green alone. bin/test had no bound at all, and its greens are reused by digest by agents. And the judge forked a child even when every test it selected was green at its digest (what the turn had just run). Changed: `seon.proofs/run-tests!` (the one runner) takes a per-test bound: each test, its :each fixtures included, runs under a Jolt `run-interruptible` budget of its own inside its :each fixtures (so their teardown runs), the namespace's fixtures within the tests' budgets and one more (`seon.proofs/test-vars`, Jolt's clojure.test fixture atoms); a test past it is an `:error` report of `:seon.test/ticks-spent` naming it and the run goes on; each result records its `:ticks`. One key, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (200e9, the old judge bound), per test, used by a turn, the judge and bin/test (test/test_runner.clj); 0 means none, read once in the runner. `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` forks nothing when nothing is left to run. Deleted: the turn's own key (config, two schema entries), the mode switch in `seon.agent/handle!`, `seon.agent/run-tests-here`'s run-level budget and its error assembly. Kept by ruling (a), 2026-10-22: what a turn selects (proofs and what the branch changed) and what the judge selects (the plan); the judge runs the plan less every green at its digest, so after a turn it runs only what the turn did not. Not changed: reds are not reused (a red reruns, to the same verdict now that the bound is the test's own); the judge still asks the agent's `:tests` op rather than `seon.prove` (its runs are not recorded on main as the turn's are). Correctness: one verdict per test and digest everywhere. Perf: a judge after a covering turn forks no child and runs no test (measured in seon.agent-test/one-bound-one-verdict-and-the-judge-reruns-no-green: :ran 0, no :pid); a turn may now do up to the per-test bound per selected test (its selection is the proofs and what changed)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/each-test-has-its-own-budget seon.agent-test/one-bound-one-verdict-and-the-judge-reruns-no-green
                      seon.agent-test/a-wide-reach-reuses-unchanged-results seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-at-its-budget-though-its-tests-run-bounded-evals}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused"
  :seon.task/plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/one-prove-path. Only greens are stored and reused by digest (`seon.proofs/store-greens!`, `seon.proofs/greens`, `seon.proofs/stored-greens`), so a red reruns at every ask: the judge after a red turn reruns every red the turn found, and so does each next turn on an unchanged tree. Since one-prove-path a test's verdict is a function of the test and its digest alone (one per-test tick bound everywhere), so a red at a digest is as reusable as a green (AGENTS section 5: a test's result is keyed by the digest of everything it reaches; a flaky red is a defect to fix at its cause, never a reason to rerun). Correct: the test service's red facts on main (`seon.prove/result-tx`: `:seon.proof/status` and `:seon.proof/failures` at `:seon.proof/digest`) are read by `seon.agent/test-plan` like greens, and a red reused is reported as red with its recorded failures, marked `:reused`. What reads 'a red runs again' today must change with it: proofs_test's shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored asserts it. Perf: a red turn's judge forks nothing for its reds. Question for root: reuse reds only from the test service's facts (recorded with failures), not from the results directory (which stores greens only)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused-with-its-failures}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-law-over-source-runs-in-prove"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the full run of main 888a728e2: three reds (seon.main-test/no-docstring-carries-a-date, seon.main-test/only-measure-reads-the-tick-counter, seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed) merged because bin/prove leaves out every test seon.program/graph-index flags :process?. The check-test one starts a seed, a real process. The main-test laws only read the compiler's facts of the tree, yet reach one: measured on the reach graph (a BFS from each test's form over :adj to a form in :proc), every law in main-test (also a-runs-lifecycle-is-read-once, a-bound-is-a-config-key) is one edge from `test-runner/program-db`, which calls clojure.java.shell/sh (a :seon.config.source/process-vars var) for `git cat-file`, `rm -rf` of an oversize store and the git archive import (tree-files). A second route, seon.test-store/program-store -> program-store-path's shutdown hook (sh), flags it too. Not the ns form, and not the :reads edge (seon.program/names-only). Every test calling program-db (program-test, proofs-test, test-store, agent-test) is left out for the same reason. Correct: the program db of the tree is built once by the runner's plan (test-runner/tests-of already builds it) and a test reads that value, not a shell; the test's digest still takes the tree (the :reads flag), so a law reruns when the source changes. Splitting program-db into a no-sh open and a shelling import is weaker (a test cannot know the branch exists). Coordinate with the printer session's reach edge (seon.program/names-only, the :reads flag). Proof: bin/prove's plan line counts the main-test laws among the tests to run, not left out; seon.proofs-test/a-run-that-names-nothing-leaves-out-what-reaches-a-process still holds for a test that starts a root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-failed-import-leaves-no-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (2026-10-25, root's two fixes): test-runner/program-db makes the tree's facts from the parent branch before it makes the tree's branch (a file that does not read throws first), and a refused transaction deletes the branch it made (a finally on a success flag, no catch); a test namespace the program db holds no test of fails the plan, named (test-runner/tests-of's :no-test), exit 1, never an empty OK. A store made before the fix may still hold a partial branch: the plan now fails on it by name. Found by form-keywords, 2026-10-25: test-runner/program-db makes the tree's branch (d/branch!) before it transacts that tree's import, so when the transact fails (here Datahike refused a :form/keywords value: datahike.transaction/a-keyword-attributes-two-values-are-two-values), the branch stays, named for the tree, empty or behind. The next plan of a changed tree branches from it as the kept tree of the fewest changed files and refreshes only those, so its program db holds only the namespaces changed since: `bin/test seon.program-test` selected 0 tests and said TEST OK, and datahike.query-test, untouched, selected 0. Fix at the producer: the branch exists only once its import committed (transact on a branch named for the tree only after, or delete the branch when the transact throws, as the one edge function the failure-points page names). Proof: a plan whose import throws leaves the store's branches as they were, and the next plan of the same program finds every test namespace."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/an-unchanged-tree-runs-nothing-and-reuses-everything seon.proofs-test/a-failed-import-leaves-no-branch}
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "mains-verification-goes-first"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's ruling (2026-10-26): main's merge verification goes first, as a fact the tools read, never a rule people remember, a timer or a poll. Seen today: main's merge verification waited an hour for the load to drop, heavy runs queued on a sleep-poll every session copied (the audit's item 10), and merge lines were typed by hand, some green over a red exit (item 5), with process tests named by hand (item 7b). Built: bin/prove --merge holds an exclusive flock on the main checkout's target/main-verify.lock for its whole run; every other bin/test, bin/check and bin/build calls bin/env's main_first, a blocking flock released at once, so it waits only while main's verification runs and says whom it waits for. The command runs the plan's :merge mode (every key with no green at its digest, process keys too), bin/check contracts and smoke, ends on MERGE OK / MERGE FAILED from their exit statuses (merge_verdict) and appends its line to notes/merge-log.md (the counts, the verdicts, the reds, the src delta, the keys left out against main's last line). docs/merging.md#main-goes-first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-heavy-run-waits-while-mains-verification-runs seon.check-test/a-merges-verdict-is-its-exit-statuses-and-it-writes-its-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reads-its-own-recorded-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused (merged 52b374f02). `seon.agent/test-plan` reuses the results the agent's own db holds (`seon.proofs/stored-results` over its branch), and the test service records a run's results on main (`seon.prove/result-tx`), so a branch sees main's results only as of its last merge of main. A red the agent's own turn just recorded is on main and not on its branch, so the judge at the same run's end (mode :judge, `seon.agent/prove!` since task seon.reconcile/the-judge-asks-the-test-service) runs it again; a green is reused from the results directory, a red never. Correct: the plan reads results from where the service writes them, main's newest value, not the branch's. Two candidates, to price first: (a) the service answers the agent's run with its result facts, and the agent applies them to its branch in the same commit as its answer (a write of facts true anywhere: they key by digest); (b) the agent's plan reads `stored-results` from main's current head, which the agent does not hold today (the fork server's replica is main at each commit: `seon.agent/base-graph`'s source). No clock, no second store of results. Proof: a turn whose proof is red, then the judge at the same digest: the judge forks nothing and reports the red with its failures, marked :reused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-judge-reuses-the-red-its-turn-recorded}} {:seon.task/name "main-is-proved-in-a-fork"
